  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 131 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThe ECMWF extreme forecast index and shift of tails show that models \nhave high confidence in an anomalous rainfall event impacting \nMissouri later this week. With PWATs forecast near their \nclimatological maximum, a deep longwave trough ejecting plenty of \nenergy into the region, and slow moving fronts stationed nearby, \nthis isn't hard to believe. Currently, ensembles and global models \nplace the axis of heaviest rainfall from Kansas northeast into Iowa \nlate Tuesday into Wednesday. This is where confidence is highest in \nheavy rainfall. To the southeast, where we reside, is where \nuncertainty in rainfall amounts grows. Although guidance is \nconsistent regarding the general pattern, there are plenty of \ndiscrepancies model to model including location, amplitude, and \nphasing of the longwave trough and therefore the footprint and \nstrength of low-level moisture advection. This isn't considering the \npotential for model under-dispersion of the tropical moisture \nor the speed at which this moisture is used up before it reaches\nour area. In short, there are multiple failure points \nconcerning this week's heavy rain potential.\n\nIf it is fully realized, localized pockets of 4+\" cannot be ruled \nout in northeast Missouri and west-central Illinois as suggested by \nthe LREF 90th percentile of total precipitation ending Saturday. \nMuch of the soil moisture in these areas falls below the 5th \npercentile of climatology, which is beneficial from a flooding \nperspective. What also helps is that the rain will be falling over \nmultiple days, so the potential for rapidly rising water appears low \nat this point. This system will continue to be closely watched as it \nevolves over the coming days.",
